 "text": "<strong>Calvin:</strong> What are you doing?<br/>\n<strong>John:</strong> I'm just studying for a test. How are you? <br/>\n<strong>Calvin:</strong> Well, I can't complain. How's the course going?<br/>\n<strong>John:</strong> I'm very happy I took this course about PHP. I was thinking that PHP is easier than Java and also many big companies prefer to use it. <br/>\n<strong> Calvin:</strong> That's true. Many well-known businesses work with PHP. You'll be working in one of them someday. If I had studied about PHP earlier, I would have obtained better jobs. There's a lot of jobs out there, so finding one is like shooting fish in a barrel. <br/>\n<strong>John:</strong> I know!  <br/>"}, {"time": "38000", "image": "enpe7n8le01ob09re2img02.jpg", "id": 1, "text": "<strong> Calvin:</strong> There are some things you have to know to master PHP, though. If I had known this before, I would have studied them in depth. For example, PHP supports different types of data. <br/>\n<strong>John:</strong> Exactly, today we studied strings. <br/>\n<strong> Calvin:</strong> That's the first type. The advantage of strings is that they can be as long or short as you want. Have you studied integers?<br/>\n<strong>John:</strong> Not yet, but I would like to know about it. <br/>\n<strong> Calvin:</strong> An integer is a data type that includes non-decimal numbers; these numbers are positive or negative and don't need a quotation mark. You just have to type them in the value. <br/>"}, {"time": "76500", "image": "enpe7n8le01ob09re2img03.jpg", "id": 2, "text": "<strong>John:</strong> There's another one that the teacher mentioned today, but I don't remember the name; it starts with an \u201cA\u201d. <br/>\n<strong> Calvin:</strong> Arrays? <br/>\n<strong>John:</strong> That's it! I was nervous because she was asking some partners about it.<br/>\n<strong> Calvin:</strong> Ha-ha! Well, if your teacher had asked you to talk about arrays, you would have mentioned that they work with multiple values. You know, they're going to be very useful when you need to arrange elements in a list. On the other hand, we also have floats; they hold a number and a decimal point. <br/>\n<strong>John:</strong> I guess I'm going to have trouble remembering every single name.<br/>\n<strong> Calvin:</strong> Don't worry! It's a piece of cake. A good piece of advice is to try to use mnemonic techniques; it works a treat. I would have memorized all these names if I had known this technique in the past. <br/>"}], "coverimg": "enpe7n8le01ob09re2img01.jpg", "audiosrc": "enpe7n8le01ob09re2aud01"}, "type": "slideshow"}]}], "type": "scholar"}]}